ely-voter polls since late August uniformly show Luján ahead by 16-18 points, every major rater places the race in the Safe or Solid Democratic column, and prediction markets price his odds near 97-98%. Marker's campaign has almost no funding and no outside support, leaving no evident path to close the gap.", "watch": "Only a late surge of outside spending against Luján or an unexpected scandal would put the margin in doubt, and neither is currently evident.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Incumbent Ben Ray Luján (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Senator Ben Ray Luján seeks a second term against Republican Larry Marker, a lightly funded challenger. Recent statewide polls of likely voters put Luján ahead by 16 to 18 points, a consistent picture across three surveys since late August. Every major race rating, from Cook and Inside Elections to Sabato and The Economist, places the contest in the Safe or Solid Democratic column, and prediction markets price Luján's chances near 97 to 98 percent.\n\nThe resource gap is stark. Luján has raised about $6.4 million and holds $3.6 million on hand, while Marker has raised roughly $92,000 with under $2,000 remaining, and no outside groups are advertising against the incumbent. New Mexico's Democratic lean and a favorable national environment reinforce the incumbent's standing. The race has drawn little attention, and no credible signal of a competitive contest has emerged.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9923, "margin": 15.81}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9906, "margin": 15.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9896, "margin": 15.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9897, "margin": 15.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9862, "margin": 15.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9853, "margin": 16.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.9894, "margin": 15.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.9908, "margin": 15.5}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.9915, "margin": 16.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.99